{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And Avraham drew near, etc.</b> Since HaShem said ״הַכְּצַעֲקָתָהּ וְגוֹ׳ עָשׂוּ כָּלָה״, this implies that when the measure of judgment tilts toward complete destruction for all, even though there are some righteous among them, therefore he said ״הַאַף תִּסְפֶּה״ etc. And even if you say that your intention is to make a way to rescue the good ones among them, as He did for Lot, whom He brought out to the outside — I still press this argument: perhaps there are fifty righteous — will You then destroy the wicked and not bear [their iniquity] for the sake of the fifty, and not remove the wicked from her midst? And this is what he was precise in saying <b>that are within her.</b> And his saying <b>far be it from You</b> etc. [addresses] the death of a righteous person, corresponding to the first claim, where he said ״הַאַף תִּסְפֶּה״. But the second claim, in which he sought to save righteous and wicked together, is nothing but a plea for mercy — perhaps HaShem will act for him and not extend the line of judgment to the very end." "And his saying <b>and it shall be as the righteous</b> etc. — meaning: when You put to death a righteous person, it will be in equalization among the created beings together, the righteous like the wicked at one level, and they will not exert themselves to become righteous." "And what he said a second time, ״far be it from You,״ corresponds to the second claim: if He is unwilling to bear [their iniquity] for the sake of fifty and destroys all the wicked — ״far be it״ etc. Another interpretation: since He had already sworn after the waters of Noah [never again to destroy all flesh], He revealed His intent that He would not extend the line of judgment to the very end. And see what I wrote in parashat Noaḥ (Genesis 9:11) in my commentary on the verse ״וַהֲקִימוֹתִי״, and from there you will understand Avraham's intention in his argument."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {0 "The ולוי יהיה in the original appears to introduce a hypothetical counterargument; rendered as 'And even if you say.'", 1 "The phrase כל הרשעים at the end of this comment is absent; the translation follows the Hebrew as given, reading the final clause as a general statement about losing motivation to act righteously."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:42:17.748025194Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}